NO!!
I stand by my solution (see article entitled: SOLUTION to negative armor
class deception;  Message-ID: <31501@lll-winken.LLNL.GOV>).
With the function I have described:

    / Ac >= 0:  f(Ac) = Ac
   {
    \ Ac <  0:  f(Ac) = -x * log(1 - Ac / x)
                where x is somewhere around 20

the improvement in effective armor class drops off SHARPLY at high (more
negative) earned armor classes (for lack of a better term).  Since
the development team can pick an appropriate x for the equation, lets pick
x = 10, for the sake of argument:
Even if the player achieves an earned armor class (Ac in the equation)
of -500, his/her effective armor class will still be only -39!
How can one become invincible without cheating?  Even with cheating,
one must achieve an Ac of -100,000 in order to even get an effective armor
class of -92!  Look at the function again.  It's exponential.

Here is the curve for x = 10:
                                 f(Ac)
                                   |       ,'
                                   |     ,'
                              -10  |   ,'
  -80 -70 -60 -50 -40 -30 -20  |-5 | ,'
  _|___|___|___|___|___|___|___|_|_|'__________ Ac
                                 ,'|__  -5
                           ,,,'''  |__ -10
                   ,,,,,,''        |
  ,,,,'''''''''''''                |__ -20
                                   |
                                   |__ -30

You CANNOT become invincible with this solution.  But you can always
improve your situation (even if by only a small amount).

